*   Inner Class in Java
             An inner class is a class that is declared inside another class.
*   Types of inner class:
             1. Member Inner Class
             2. Static Nested Class
             3. Local Inner Class
             4. Anonymous Inner Class

*   A MEMBER INNER class is a non-static class declared directly inside another class, at the class/member level.
             If the inner class needs the outer class object's data → use a Member Inner Class.
             To access a inner class or to create a object of inner member class first we need to create the object of outer class
             and after the with reference of outer class object we can create object of inner class
             Can directly access Outer class's instance variables/methods
             Used when inner object is strongly connected to a specific outer object

*   STATIC INNER class is directly belongs to the outer class rather than its instance/object
             To access a inner static class we do not need the object of outer class
             We can directly create the object of inner static class to access it
             Used when inner class is related to Outer class but doesn't need a specific Outer object
             Cannot directly access Outer class's instance variables/methods
             and if static inner class want to access outer class variables/methods can access with the help of outer class object but it is not needed as we can do so with the help of member inner class also

*   ANNONYMOUS INNER CLASS -  An Anonymous Inner Class is a class that is declared and instantiated at the same time, without giving the class a name. It is mainly used when we need a one-time implementation of an interface, abstract class, or an existing concrete class, without creating a separate named class.
              General Syntax
              Type reference = new Type() {

                  // fields

                  // methods

              };

              Here:

              Type can be an interface, abstract class, or concrete class.
              new Type() → creates the anonymous class object. type can be name of that class interface anything
              { } → contains the implementation/overridden methods.
              There is no class name, hence "anonymous".
              The main reason we use it is:

              When we need a small, one-time implementation and don't want to create a separate named class.

*    LOCAL INNER CLASS - Local Inner Class

                         A Local Inner Class is a non-static class declared inside a method, constructor, or block of another class.

                         Because it is declared inside a local scope, it is called a local inner class.

